          { name: "College-Educated Voter Expectations & Education Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 36.4% college attainment, SC-HD-6 has the highest college-educated share in many state districts—a constituency capable of evaluating complex policy trade-offs and attuned to school funding, higher education accessibility, and workforce development specifics. This is not a base that responds to abstract conservative messaging; it responds to concrete evidence of educational investment and quality. Visibility on education policy, higher education funding, and the district's school performance metrics is essential to maintaining the college-educated majority's confidence." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap Engagement (5.25% Unaccounted Population)", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 94.75% of the district, leaving approximately 5.25% unaccounted for—roughly 1 in 20 constituents. In a district of this size, this gap likely represents Black, Hispanic, Asian, or Native American residents whose civic and economic concerns may not be fully visible in the 74% partisan result. While this constituency is not large enough to change the overall margin, active engagement demonstrates governance competence and prevents a future challenger from building a targeted mobilization campaign among these voters." },
          { name: "Treating 74% as a Permanent Durable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74% result reflects successful current consolidation, not permanent immunity to challenge. The district's economic foundation—$77,559 median income, affluent but not wealthy, older with fixed costs—is vulnerable to recession, healthcare inflation, property tax increases, or education quality deterioration. Any candidate who governs with the confidence that 74% is a locked-in floor rather than a result that must be actively defended through constituent service and responsive governance is exposing the seat to a credible future challenge." },
          { name: "Dismissing or Minimizing Non-Anglo Constituent Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "With 83.58% white residents, SC-HD-6 is a heavily Anglo district—but 16.42% of constituents are not, and they deserve specific attention rather than absorption into generic constituent service. Any pattern of governance or communication that treats Black, Hispanic, Asian, or Native American residents as peripheral to the district's primary concerns signals to those voters that they are not valued constituents. In a district this affluent and secure, such neglect is both ethically indefensible and strategically risky if a future challenger targets these communities with evidence of consistent exclusion." }
