          { name: "Renter Housing Affordability and Tenant Rights", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 23.4% renter rate in a working-class district represents roughly 3,500-4,500 households who are likely experiencing acute housing cost burden relative to their $58,660-median income. Renters in working-class districts have historically lower electoral participation than homeowners, which may explain the 87-13 supermajority — they are simply not mobilized in current electoral contests. A challenger who leads on tenant protections, rental cost regulation, and renter-specific housing affordability programs could activate this constituency with direct material appeals if current representation is perceived as favoring homeowner interests over renter welfare." },
          { name: "Black Community Political Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 18.48% Black constituency represents roughly 7,000-8,500 residents in SC-HD-8. An 87-13 result in a district with this Black population share requires investigation into whether Black voters are actually aligned with that margin or whether lower political engagement among Black voters is masking latent opposition. If Black political participation is substantially lower than white participation — a common pattern in working-class rural districts — then the 87-13 result is inflated by differential turnout, not genuine consensus. A challenger with authentic credibility in the Black community on criminal justice, economic opportunity, and healthcare access could shift the partisan balance substantially if Black electoral participation increases." },
          { name: "Dismissing 87% as Durable Republican Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "An 87-13 result reflects incumbent advantage and possible opposition disengagement, not necessarily genuine constituent satisfaction. In a working-class district with material economic pressures and a 23.4% renter population with historically lower participation, the supermajority may be fragile — vulnerable to a challenger who mobilizes renters, lower-income homeowners with healthcare cost pressures, or a Black constituency that is currently underparticipating. Any candidate who treats this margin as a stable floor rather than an artifact of incumbent advantage and opposition disengagement is strategically misreading the district." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Material Concerns Beneath a Supermajority Result", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where median income is working-class, college attainment is below state average, healthcare costs are rising faster than wages in service sectors, and renters represent nearly a quarter of the population, focusing on abstract partisan messaging rather than concrete policy solutions to material economic problems is strategically inert. An incumbent who assumes the 87-13 result means these concerns do not exist is vulnerable to a challenger who proves otherwise with specific policy commitments and credible local engagement on healthcare affordability, housing costs, and wage stability." }
