          { name: "Middle-Income Suburban Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $69,951 median income and 38.5-year median age with 74.7% homeownership, HD-10 is a peak-mortgage-burden, dual-income household constituency facing tangible financial pressure from healthcare costs, property taxes, and childcare expenses. The unopposed 2024 victory does not indicate that voters have evaluated the incumbent's performance on these material concerns—it indicates they were not offered an alternative. A representative who leads on healthcare cost containment, property tax fairness, and public school funding quality speaks directly to how most families in this district experience government's impact on their monthly budgets." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Growth & Persuadability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 11.15% Hispanic population in a suburban middle-income district represents a community whose political allegiance is not yet settled and whose economic concerns around housing affordability, wage growth, and access to quality services align with broader suburban household anxieties. Hispanic homeowners and renters in this income range are genuinely swing voters—not reliably Democratic but also not reflexively Republican. Authentic engagement with Spanish-language outreach, specific constituent services, and economic opportunity messaging builds a relationship that can insulate this community from recruitment by a future Democratic opponent." },
          { name: "The Risk of Unopposed Assumption", tag: "careful", why: "An unopposed 2024 victory is not evidence of durable electoral safety—it is evidence that no credible opposition was mounted. With 28.1% college attainment, 38.5 median age, and a 25.3% renter rate reflecting economic diversity, HD-10 has the demographic ingredients for competitive contestation if a well-resourced Democratic candidate emerges and targets Hispanic homeowners, younger renters, and households anxious about healthcare and education costs. The current 100% margin teaches nothing about how voters would choose if presented with a genuine alternative." },
          { name: "Ideological Positioning Over Economic Substance", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-10's voters are middle-income homeowners and renters evaluating government on whether it reduces or adds to their financial stress—not on abstract conservative or liberal principles. A representative who campaigns on ideology rather than concrete answers to healthcare costs, property tax management, and school funding quality will find that frame disconnected from how most constituents actually experience their relationship with government." },
          { name: "Neglecting Minority Communities", tag: "avoid", why: "With 11.15% Hispanic, 10.26% Black, and 1.2% Asian populations and an unopposed electoral history that precluded real competition for their support, minority communities' actual governance needs and political preferences remain untested and potentially unheard. A representative who assumes these communities will remain loyal without active constituent service and authentic engagement is creating the conditions for a future challenger to successfully organize them around specific issues like healthcare access and economic opportunity." }
