          { name: "Black Community Civic Engagement and Authentic Constituent Relationship", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 47.78% Black population and 48% Democratic opposition in a 52-48 Republican margin, Black voter persuasion is not only available but actively occurring; this is not a district where demographic categories determine political outcomes. The strength of Black community institutions — churches, civic organizations, civil rights networks, and community anchors — is the practical substrate where constituent relationships are built and voter persuasion happens. A candidate who is known, trusted, and genuinely engaged in Black community institutions, including serving on boards, attending regularly, and being responsive to specific community concerns, will command more durable support than one who campaigns episodically or treats the Black community as a monolithic voting bloc." },
          { name: "Understanding the 48% Opposition Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "The 48% Democratic share in a 47.78% Black district is the single most important analytical fact about SC-HD-12: it proves that Black voters are not monolithically aligned and that significant portions of the community were persuaded by the Democratic candidate. The incumbent cannot assume that Black demographic plurality guarantees political support; understanding specifically whether the 48% opposition is concentrated among younger voters frustrated by economic opportunity, renters squeezed by housing costs, lower-income households evaluating healthcare access, or a combination is essential for holding the seat. Any incumbent campaign that relies on demographic loyalty rather than active constituent persuasion will find that assumption tested in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Treating 52% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result is not a majority — it is a four-point plurality in a district that is nearly half Black and contains substantial Democratic opposition. In a working-class community where economic concerns drive voter behavior rather than inherited partisan loyalty, a margin this thin is vulnerable to credible challenger performance on jobs, housing, and healthcare. Any incumbent campaign that consolidates the existing Republican coalition without actively persuading the 48% opposition and addressing the economic concerns that generated it will find the seat at genuine risk in a more competitive cycle or against a stronger challenger." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging Over Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 16.5% college attainment signals a constituency oriented toward immediate material concerns rather than abstract ideological positioning, and where 48% voted for the opposition despite demographic factors that might predict otherwise, partisan theater will underperform relative to authentic constituent service and concrete economic proposals. Any candidate who leads with partisan attack rather than answers to questions about jobs, housing costs, healthcare access, and whether government is delivering for working people is strategically misreading the electorate and leaving persuasion opportunity on the table." }
