          { name: "Economic Competence & Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "SC-HD-20's $76,415 median income and 47.7% college attainment describe a constituency of professionals and business owners whose voting is rooted in whether government delivers measurable results on their economic concerns—tax fairness, school quality, housing affordability, and business climate. A representative who can articulate and deliver on specific legislative agenda items that address these material interests transforms the 68% from a partisan baseline into a durable majority built on demonstrated competence. Neglecting this dimension treats the seat as safely Republican when it is actually held through the incumbent's responsiveness to constituent priorities." },
          { name: "Minority Community Economic Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 12.6% Hispanic/Latino population is SC-HD-20's second-largest single demographic group, and Black, Asian, and other communities together represent roughly 13% of the district—making minority communities a mathematically significant swing constituency in a 68-32 seat. In districts with this income and education profile, minority professionals and entrepreneurs are evaluable on economic opportunity: small business capital access, professional hiring and advancement, and entrepreneurship support are the specific issues most likely to shift voting patterns among college-educated minorities. A representative who engages these communities with genuine economic policy rather than cultural positioning builds a stronger, more durable majority." },
          { name: "Renter Constituency & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "At 24.9%, renters represent a meaningful minority (roughly one-quarter) of SC-HD-20's residents, and in an environment of rising housing costs across South Carolina this constituency is persuadable and likely to become more politically active if housing affordability continues to deteriorate. The renter population in a $76,415-income district is likely to include younger professionals, service workers, and families priced out of ownership—constituencies with different material concerns than homeowners. An incumbent who acknowledges renter concerns on rental market policy, affordable housing access, and residential stability avoids creating an organized opposition constituency around a bread-and-butter issue." },
          { name: "Treating 68% as a Durable Safe Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "A 68% result in a district with significant demographic diversity, college-educated swing voters, and professional constituencies that evaluate on economic outcomes is not a mandate—it is a strong plurality that requires active constituent engagement and demonstrated legislative effectiveness to hold. The 32% opposition in a district this affluent and educated is likely to include persuadable professionals and business owners who will support a credible challenger if the incumbent becomes unresponsive to their specific economic concerns. Any representative who treats this seat as safe and directs energy elsewhere rather than strengthening relationships with professional and minority constituencies is arithmetically vulnerable." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Diversity Beneath the White Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "While white residents represent 71.27% of SC-HD-20, the remaining 28.73%—including 12.6% Hispanic/Latino, 8.87% Black, 4.41% Asian, and other communities—is large enough that an incumbent's visibility and responsiveness to these communities directly affects electoral durability. A representative who operates as though the 68% is driven entirely by white homeowners and treats minority communities as peripheral rather than as integral constituencies is missing the swing voters most likely to organize an alternative if they feel unserved. In a high-income, college-educated district, minority professionals have economic concerns and civic participation rates comparable to white constituents, and they will evaluate and move their votes based on legislative responsiveness." }
