          "The 40% Democratic opposition, however, contains an analytical signal worth examining: in a district this prosperous and college-educated, opposition at 40% suggests the Democrat is capturing not just the renter population and younger adults, but also a meaningful share of college-educated homeowners who are departing the Republican coalition. This is the demographic signature of education-driven partisan realignment — college-educated professionals whose economic security is stable but whose social or institutional values are shifting away from Republican positioning. For Wickensimer, the strategic task is not to mobilize a base (the homeowner, income-affluent, professionally established core is already consolidated) but to understand what is driving college-educated defection and whether it represents a durable ideological shift or a response to specific local or state-level policies that can be addressed. A 60-40 margin in an educated, affluent district is secure only if the incumbent actively engages the 40% who chose differently — not to convert them, necessarily, but to understand whether the opposition reflects real constituent concern about governance priorities or is simply partisan migration among college-educated voters nationwide."
