          { name: "Renter-Centered Housing and Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 70.1% renters with $40,846 median household income, the constituency is overwhelmingly economically dependent on housing affordability and tenant protections rather than homeownership equity. Rising rents, eviction risk, and neighborhood displacement are direct household economic shocks — not abstract policy concerns — for a majority of constituents. A legislative agenda centered on tenant rights, rent stabilization, eviction prevention, and equitable development that prioritizes existing renters over displacement is the foundation for sustaining the 80% Democratic coalition. Without active legislative attention to these material concerns, renter households will perceive the representative as serving other constituencies and will withhold turnout support in subsequent cycles." },
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Opportunity and Wage Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 30.5% college-attainment rate and $40,846 median income indicate a workforce concentrated in service, trade, and lower-wage positions without substantial white-collar employment options. Minimum wage policy, wage theft enforcement, job training access, and childcare affordability are the concrete governance priorities that directly affect household earning potential and economic mobility. In a district where renters and working-age adults dominate, a representative who demonstrates measurable legislative effort on wage and employment policy builds credibility with the constituency that is actually most economically vulnerable and most responsive to legislative action on these issues." },
          { name: "Demographic Coalition Composition and Base Turnout", tag: "careful", why: "The district's near-parity racial composition — 40.43% white, 39% Black, 16.15% Hispanic — means the 80% Democratic result requires both strong performance among Black voters and solid margins among white and Hispanic working-class constituents. Changes in turnout within any demographic group, or shifts in intra-group candidate preference, are capable of reducing the 80% significantly. Systematic tracking of whether turnout among renters, younger voters, and each racial group is consistent with baseline expectations is essential to early detection of coalition fragmentation. Assuming the 80% is demographically stable without evidence is a high-risk posture in a plurality-composition district." },
          { name: "Treating 80% as a Structural Majority Rather Than an Active Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "An 80-20 result is an electoral outcome of the current cycle, not a permanent demographic structure — particularly in a district where 70% are renters, median age is 34.7 (economically mobile), and the racial composition is near-parity rather than a clear majority. A representative who assumes the 80% will persist without ongoing legislative investment in the material concerns of renters, working-class households, and each demographic group will find the coalition fragmenting when the next competitive election arrives or the incumbent is absent." },
          { name: "Allowing Other Demographic Groups' Specific Concerns to Become Invisible Within a Generalized 'Democratic Coalition' Narrative", tag: "avoid", why: "With Black, white, and Hispanic constituencies each representing substantive shares of the population (39%, 40.43%, 16.15% respectively), collapsing their distinct economic and social priorities into a generic Democratic-base message risks alienating specific groups whose engagement drives turnout. Black voters' concerns with racial equity in hiring and housing, Hispanic voters' concerns with immigration status and workplace protections, and white working-class voters' concerns with economic mobility and job access are not interchangeable — they require distinct constituent attention. A representative who fails to demonstrate specific policy engagement with each group's actual priorities will find support fragmenting across candidates and turnout declining among constituencies who feel generically acknowledged but specifically ignored." }
