        memoHeadline: "SC-HD-25: A 63-37 Democratic Hold Built on a 46% Black Majority, Low Educational Attainment, and Economic Vulnerability—Where the Incumbent's Margin Masks Structural Fragility in a Working-Class, Renter-Heavy Constituency.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "SC-HD-25 is a heavily Democratic district on the surface—Jones won 63-37 in 2024—but the demographic and economic profile beneath that margin reveals a constituency that is materially stressed and electorally fragile if challenger messaging resonates with kitchen-table concerns. The district is 46.37% Black, 32.59% white, and 16.83% Hispanic, with a significant remainder (~4%) likely American Indian or multi-racial. At $51,378 median household income, 17.1% college attainment, and 38.3% renter rate, SC-HD-25 is a working-class, education-constrained, rent-burdened community where economic precarity is the lived reality for a substantial portion of the constituency. The 36.8-year median age signals a district with young families and working-age adults whose economic mobility is directly threatened by housing costs, healthcare access, and job quality—issues that transcend partisan loyalty if a challenger offers concrete material relief.",
          "The 63% margin is real and reflects genuine Democratic consolidation among Black voters and working-class households, but it is not a safe harbor if the incumbent becomes associated with governance failures on the material concerns that dominate this constituency's daily life. With 38.3% renters—above state median—housing affordability, tenant protections, and eviction prevention are not abstract policy concerns but survival issues for more than one-third of households. Similarly, the 17.1% college attainment rate is the lowest in any comparative analysis and signals limited pathways to economic mobility for young adults in the district; education access, workforce training, and job creation in higher-wage sectors are the currency of genuine constituent service. A Democratic incumbent holding 63% in a working-class, rent-heavy, low-education district has built a durable coalition—but only if that coalition perceives the representative as actively fighting for their material economic security. Any perception of distance from these concerns, or messaging that emphasizes identity or cultural issues over kitchen-table economics, creates an opening for a challenger who leads with concrete relief proposals on housing, healthcare, and economic opportunity."
        ],
