          { name: "Hispanic Community Civic Participation & Voter Mobilization", tag: "careful", why: "At 9.84%, the Hispanic population represents approximately one in ten district voters—a proportion sufficient to shift outcomes in a competitive race if mobilization and turnout increase. Hispanic voter participation in South Carolina state house races has been less robust than in neighboring states, but this represents opportunity for candidates who invest in Spanish-language outreach and visible engagement with Hispanic community institutions. The incumbent should not assume this population's electoral behavior is fixed; a challenger who demonstrates fluent Spanish-language competence and specific policy attention could meaningfully engage this constituency." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Structural Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed result reflects Republican recruitment weakness or strategic choice, not voter loyalty to Montgomery or structural Democratic strength. In a district with 38.3% renters at $61,487 median income and meaningful Black and Hispanic populations, a well-resourced Republican challenger with credible local standing and working-class economic messaging could plausibly contest this seat. The absence of opposition in 2024 should trigger protective work, not complacency." },
          { name: "Assuming Black Voter Loyalty Without Active Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "The 21.73% Black population in a district where Republicans are beginning to compete for working-class voters cannot be held through demographic alignment alone. Without continuous, visible engagement on criminal justice, education investment, and economic opportunity—demonstrated through responsive constituent service, visible leadership in Black community institutions, and transparent accountability for outcomes—an incumbent risks losing this population to a credible challenger who offers authentic engagement as an alternative." }
