          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Status as Permanent", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed victory is a fact, not a forecast: Democratic non-competition may reflect resource constraints, weak bench strength, or strategic prioritization elsewhere rather than genuine uncompetitiveness in the district. A 33.9% college-educated, $76,534-median-income suburban district is precisely the type of seat where demographic shifts, mobilization of renter and educated-voter constituencies, or a high-profile local issue could generate credible Democratic opposition in 2026 or 2028. An incumbent who treats 100% as permission to neglect constituency service or become inattentive to emerging concerns will be genuinely vulnerable if opposition materializes." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Culture-War Messaging in an Educated, Economically Diverse District", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated voters (33.9% attainment) and renters (19.9%) respond to messaging that prioritizes education quality, healthcare access, and economic mobility over culture-war framing, even in Republican districts. An incumbent whose primary messaging centers on divisive social issues rather than education outcomes, healthcare cost control, or housing affordability is misaligning with the district's actual demographic composition. Democratic opposition would exploit this mismatch relentlessly, positioning the incumbent as out of touch with constituents' material priorities." }
