          { name: "Economic Security for Working-to-Middle-Class Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $60,788 median household income and 24.5% college attainment, SC-HD-40 is a working-to-middle-class constituency managing mortgage, healthcare, and family expenses without significant surplus. Property tax sustainability, school funding adequacy, job availability at living wages, and healthcare access are the material governance issues that determine household economic stability. A representative who delivers on these issues across racial lines — rather than defaulting to abstract ideology — builds durable political support across the 61% white majority and 39% minority communities that share these practical concerns." },
          { name: "Active Engagement of Black and Hispanic Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 27.75% Black and 8.66% Hispanic population, combined with 25.3% renter rate and modest college attainment, indicates minority communities that are economically vulnerable and likely concentrated in lower-income households relative to the white majority. Unopposed status has allowed the current representative to govern without demonstrating investment in these communities' material concerns or building earned trust. A future challenger who mobilizes these communities around specific economic demands — housing, healthcare, job quality — can activate a dormant 38.8% coalition that constitutes a majority, making this a genuinely contested seat rather than a default Republican hold." },
          { name: "The Structural Risk of Unopposed Governance", tag: "careful", why: "Unopposed status in 2024 reflects absence of immediate organized challenge, not invulnerability. In a district where 38.8% of residents are Black, Hispanic, or other minorities, and where economic conditions for working-to-middle-class households are neither uniformly improving nor uniformly secure, the absence of a challenger is likely explained by resource constraints or strategic choices of would-be opponents rather than genuine political satisfaction. Any incumbent who treats unopposed status as evidence of durable security rather than a window of opportunity to build invested support among minority communities and economically vulnerable households is misreading the district's actual political landscape." },
          { name: "Assuming Default White Republican Support", tag: "avoid", why: "The 61.2% white majority does not automatically translate to solid Republican performance if economic conditions deteriorate, schools underperform, or the incumbent fails to deliver on working-class economic security issues. White working-to-middle-class voters at $60,788 income are not ideologically locked into Republican affiliation if they perceive government as failing to serve their practical interests. Treating white constituents as a secured base and ignoring economic delivery is a recipe for vulnerable incumbent status." },
          { name: "Neglecting to Build Constituent Relationships in the 38.8% Minority Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed representative can drift into governing for the comfortable majority without actively investing in the material concerns of Black, Hispanic, and other minority communities. This creates a structural opening for a challenger who commits to these communities' specific economic demands and builds a turnout operation around them. In a district where minority communities comprise more than one-third of the population, neglecting active engagement and legislative delivery is equivalent to leaving a latent majority coalition unmobilized and available for activation by an organized opponent." }
