          { name: "29.5% Black Population Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "Nearly 30% of SC-HD-42 is Black, yet the 68-32 Republican margin suggests either low Black turnout or low Black Republican support — or both. This is a constituency that is present, substantial, and currently underrepresented in the incumbent's governing coalition. Healthcare access, economic mobility, criminal justice, and local service delivery are the issues most likely to drive Black voter participation and candidate preference. A strategic outreach effort on these specific issues — not generic minority outreach, but concrete engagement on the governance concerns Black residents identify as priorities — can either increase constituent satisfaction among existing voters or create an opening for a challenger who does this work first." },
          { name: "The 4.8% Demographic Gap", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for 95.2% of the district's population, leaving 4.8% unaccounted for — likely Native American, multiracial, or other heritage communities. While 4.8% is modest as a percentage, it represents a real group of constituents whose specific governance concerns may not be receiving legislative attention. In a district where the margin is 68-32, a 4.8% constituency is not large enough to flip the seat, but it is large enough that neglecting it creates a perception of incomplete representation and leaves room for a challenger to build coalition with voters the incumbent has overlooked." },
          { name: "Taking 68% as a Permanent Electoral Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 68-32 result in a working-class homeowner district can create false confidence that the seat is secure regardless of constituent service quality. The 32% opposition in a district where homeownership is dominant, median age is 42, and partisan alignment should be strong suggests either that some homeowners defected from the Republican base or that non-homeowner populations are consolidated in opposition. Treating this margin as a mandate to reduce constituent service or to neglect underengaged populations is the path to erosion — the next competitive cycle will shrink this margin if the incumbent becomes perceived as taking the base for granted." },
          { name: "Ideological Positioning Over Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "With only 17.3% college attainment, SC-HD-42 is a constituency that evaluates candidates on concrete delivery — jobs, taxes, services, whether government works for them — rather than ideological purity or abstract principle. A candidate who campaigns on ideological grounds rather than on specific answers to the economic concerns of working homeowners and underengaged minority populations is misreading the constituency. This is not a district where partisan messaging or cultural conservatism substitutes for governance results; it is a district where material economic security is the primary voting concern." }
