          "Engage the 37.5% white constituency with economic messaging that speaks to shared working-class concerns rather than racial divides: housing costs, job accessibility, and public service quality affect both the Black majority and white minority when median income is this constrained. Governing a majority-Black district successfully means building the economic case that benefits the whole district.",
          "Do not treat unopposed status as a durable political position: the absence of opposition in 2024 reflects recruitment or strategic calculus, not immovable Democratic dominance. The next challenger will target working-class economic anxiety if the incumbent's record does not show material improvement in housing stability, wage growth, or job accessibility.",
          "Prioritize constituent service and visible local investment in housing, workforce development, and small-business support: in a district where 42% of households rent and median income is $53,406, the incumbent's perceived commitment to improving material living conditions is the most reliable foundation for holding the seat against future challenge."
