          { name: "Constituent Service Infrastructure & Responsiveness", tag: "lean-into", why: "The unopposed 2024 result creates a critical opportunity to build visible constituent service capacity and relationships before the next competitive cycle emerges. Professional suburban voters reward accessibility and responsiveness—town halls, constituent service offices, clear communication on how to reach the representative, and demonstrable follow-up on constituent concerns are the operational foundations of a durable suburban seat. A representative who establishes early and clear constituent service systems signals competence and builds goodwill that translates directly into electoral durability." },
          { name: "Monitoring for Credible Democratic Competition", tag: "careful", why: "The unopposed status in 2024 is almost certainly a function of Democratic resource and organizational constraints in South Carolina, not permanent voter satisfaction with McGinnis. A credible Democratic challenger with strong credentials on suburban governance issues—education funding, tax fairness, infrastructure—could quickly make this seat competitive if organized effectively. The incumbent should treat the unopposed result not as license for complacency but as a window to build sufficient constituent relationships and legislative record that even a well-funded challenger faces significant barriers." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Stability Implies Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "Suburban districts in the United States have proven volatile in recent cycles, with voter behavior driven more by incumbent performance and challenger quality than by demographic composition alone. A 77% white, affluent, college-educated suburban constituency is not inherently or permanently Republican in electoral behavior; it is a constituency that evaluates competence and has shifted dramatically in recent cycles when incumbents underperformed or challengers offered credible alternatives. McGinnis's task is not to assume the demographics do the work; it is to build a constituent relationship so strong that demographic composition becomes secondary." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Abstract Conservative Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "Professional suburban voters are not activated by or particularly responsive to abstract conservative ideology; they are activated by specific governance outcomes that affect their material interests. A representative who campaigns or legislates primarily on cultural or abstract ideological issues while neglecting school funding, infrastructure investment, or tax policy that affects their property values and daily lives is actively misunderstanding the constituency. The 35.9% college-educated population especially evaluates on substance and competence rather than partisan positioning, making generic conservative messaging actively ineffective in this district." }
