        issues: [
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security and Wage Stagnation", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $66,615 median household income with 27.4% college attainment and a 38.8 median age, SC-HD-61 is a working-class district where wage growth, job quality, and cost-of-living pressure are the primary household concerns. Unlike affluent districts where taxation and regulation dominate voter priorities, this constituency evaluates whether government is actually making their economic lives more stable or more precarious. A legislative record that demonstrates specific attention to wage policy, manufacturing competitiveness, vocational training, and small-business support is the mechanism to build durable voter loyalty in a district this economically vulnerable." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter-Community Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 30.3% renter rate is substantially above typical rural South Carolina levels, indicating either urban or suburban characteristics within the district or a meaningful cohort of working-class renters unable to access homeownership at current housing costs. Renters lack the property-tax and homeowner-association infrastructure through which Republicans traditionally consolidate support, making them both organizationally disadvantaged and electorally persuadable on candidate merit. Explicit legislative focus on fair-housing enforcement, rental-market transparency, and housing-cost mitigation is both a direct constituent service obligation and a mechanism to prevent Democratic opposition from organizing renters into an active bloc." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Rural Service Viability", tag: "careful", why: "The district's working-class profile and modest income suggest meaningful exposure to healthcare-cost anxiety, particularly among older adults approaching Medicare eligibility and younger families navigating private insurance in the individual market. Whether SC-HD-61 has rural healthcare challenges specifically (hospital distance, rural provider shortages) or is suburban/urban with market-based healthcare access requires district-specific intelligence, but the income and age profile suggests healthcare sustainability and cost control are constituent concerns that generate electoral vulnerability if addressed only through partisan rhetoric rather than specific policy engagement." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed 2024 Result as a Durable Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed race produces zero information about voter enthusiasm, issue prioritization, or latent opposition strength—only that Democratic resources or recruitment capacity failed in this particular cycle. Any incumbent or challenger who reads a 100-0 unopposed result as evidence of secure tenure in a district with modest income, significant renter population, and working-class economic vulnerability is misreading the strategic landscape. The 2026 cycle will reveal actual voter sentiment; preparation for serious opposition is the prudent strategic posture." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Messaging in a District Evaluating on Material Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "SC-HD-61's working-class profile, modest educational attainment, and renter-inclusive demographics indicate a constituency that evaluates candidates primarily on whether they are delivering concrete improvements to economic security, housing affordability, and healthcare access rather than on partisan identity or ideological positioning. A legislative record or campaign message built primarily around conservative ideology, cultural grievance, or partisan opposition rather than specific material governance will underperform with a constituency that needs to see actual results in their household budgets." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "SC-HD-61: An Unopposed Incumbent in a Working-Class, Middle-Aged District with Latent Democratic Vulnerability Below the Surface",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "SC-HD-61 presents a strategic paradox: Schuessler won unopposed in 2024, yet the district's demographic profile suggests more competitive terrain than the 100-0 result implies. The median household income of $66,615, college attainment of only 27.4%, median age of 38.8, and 30.3% renter rate paint a picture of a working-class to lower-middle-class community where economic security concerns and housing affordability are lived experiences, not abstract policy debates. The 72.23% white majority and 11.7% Black population signal a racially diverse district by South Carolina standards, but one where the demographic data accounts for only ~95% of the population—suggesting a ~5% gap likely captured in American Indian or multiracial categories. An unopposed race produces no data about whether this constituency is genuinely consolidated or simply unrepresented by organized opposition.",
          "The absence of a Democratic challenger in 2024 should not be read as evidence of durable Republican strength; it may reflect Democratic resource constraints, recruitment failure, or strategic abandonment rather than authentic voter satisfaction. With a median age of 38.8 and a 30.3% renter rate, SC-HD-61 contains a meaningful contingent of younger adults and non-homeowners for whom property taxes, wage stagnation, rental costs, and economic mobility are direct financial concerns. The 27.4% college attainment rate—below state and regional averages—suggests limited educational infrastructure or outmigration of degree-holders, creating a potential cohort of working-class adults without four-year credentials whose economic anxiety may not automatically track partisan identity. Any challenger who can articulate concrete solutions to housing affordability, wage growth, healthcare access, and economic opportunity in a district this economically stressed has a viable opening, particularly if Republicans take the unopposed 2024 result as permission to neglect constituent service or local governance attention."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Treat unopposed status as a vulnerability signal rather than a mandate: the lack of opposition in 2024 reflects Democratic weakness, not voter enthusiasm. An incumbent who campaigns as though the seat is safe rather than preparing for organized opposition in 2026 is at genuine risk of losing to a credible challenger who highlights specific economic concerns.",
          "Lead with working-class economic security: at $66,615 median household income with 30.3% renters and 27.4% college attainment, this is a constituency evaluating whether government is delivering on wages, housing affordability, and healthcare access. Specific policy positions on rental market regulation, wage policy, and healthcare costs are more persuasive than partisan framing.",
          "Address the renter-majority contingent directly: 30.3% renter rate in a district with relatively modest median income means significant households lack property-tax protection and are vulnerable to housing-cost inflation. Renters' concerns about fair housing, eviction protection, and housing affordability deserve explicit legislative attention rather than incorporation into homeowner-focused messaging.",
          "Build constituent service infrastructure that reaches beyond the homeowner majority: with ~30% renters and a working-class income profile, meaningful portions of the district may not be represented in traditional Republican coalition spaces (civic organizations, homeowner associations, church leadership). Direct constituent engagement at scale is the mechanism to prevent challenger recruitment and consolidate actual voter loyalty."
        ]
