          { name: "Suppressing the 19% Democratic Alternative", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district this wealthy and educated, 19% Democratic support represents a meaningful opposition infrastructure and a constituency actively available to persuasion. These voters are not marginal—they reflect genuine partisan division within the professional, college-educated class that SC-HD-69 represents, and their presence signals real space for a credible Democratic challenger to gain traction if the Republican incumbent appears to be neglecting their concerns or misallocating constituent resources. The incumbent's primary strategic task is not celebrating the 81%, but understanding specifically what drives the 19% and either persuading them or preventing a well-funded challenger from credibly claiming to represent their interests more effectively." },
          { name: "Invisible Constituency Activation in the 4.6% Demographic Gap", tag: "careful", why: "The 4.6% gap between reported Census categories and 100% population in an educated, higher-income district may represent mixed-race families, recent immigrants with business interests, or other constituencies whose presence and needs are real but whose representation in district messaging and constituent service may be minimal or tokenized. These populations often evaluate on specific governance issues affecting their economic mobility or family well-being rather than abstract partisan positioning, making them persuadable swing voters if a challenger offers credible engagement while the incumbent remains inattentive. In an 81%-19% district, activating an otherwise invisible 3-4% of the population around specific governance concerns could shift the outcome measurably." },
          { name: "Treating 81% as Structural Rather Than Earned", tag: "avoid", why: "The assumption that an 81% result represents a durable floor rather than a margin earned through active constituent engagement and effective governance is the single most dangerous strategic error an incumbent in SC-HD-69 can make. In a constituency this educated and this affluent, voters actively evaluate on substance, and any perception that the incumbent is coasting on a margin rather than working to earn it creates space for credible challenger recruitment and gradual erosion of the professional, independent voters who are capable of crossing party lines." },
          { name: "Messaging That Takes the 19% Opposition for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "Any campaign messaging that treats the 19% Democratic constituency as permanently locked out, morally illegitimate, or not worth engaging actively surrenders the opportunity to prevent a credible challenger from credibly claiming to represent those voters' interests. In a college-educated district where roughly one in five voters is actively Democratic, assuming they are not persuadable and not listening abandons the rhetorical space where electoral competition actually happens and allows the opposition to define what the incumbent's governance represents to professional and civic-minded voters who have not yet made a final choice." }
