          { name: "Professional & Educational Workforce Policy Leadership", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 47% college-attainment rate and $72,477 income in a district of educators, healthcare professionals, social workers, and government employees mean the representative can convert constituent expertise into policy leadership. Teachers in the district have direct knowledge of education funding adequacy; healthcare workers understand system constraints; public employees see governance effectiveness from inside. A representative who builds relationships with these professional networks and brings their expertise into legislative work gains both constituent validation and policy effectiveness that typical legislators lack. This is a comparative advantage available only in districts with this demographic profile, and squandering it through disengagement represents opportunity cost." },
          { name: "Maintaining Constituent Engagement Across a Secured Seat", tag: "careful", why: "A 75% Democratic result in a demographically coherent, educated, professional district can create the false impression that the seat is electorally secured and does not require active constituent engagement. In reality, a constituency this educated and economically stable is also more likely to mount a primary challenge against a representative perceived as ineffective, unresponsive, or failing to deliver on education, healthcare, or justice-system priorities. The 75% is the product of continuous constituent service and legislative effectiveness, not an electoral floor; without active attention to whether the district perceives its representative as working effectively on its priorities, the apparent security can erode quickly, particularly to a primary challenger from within the community." },
          { name: "Treating Constituent Expectations as Partisan Loyalty", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with 47% college education and a professional workforce evaluates on outcomes, not party affiliation. A representative who operates as though the 75% Democratic result means constituents will support them regardless of legislative effectiveness, constituent responsiveness, or progress on education, healthcare, and justice priorities will find this assumption actively contradicted. The educated Black professional constituency that anchors this district is capable of sustained critique and primary challenge if they conclude the representative is not delivering." },
          { name: "Ignoring Constituent Expertise & Professional Networks", tag: "avoid", why: "With educators, healthcare workers, social service professionals, and government employees comprising a substantial share of the constituency, a representative who does not engage these networks as sources of policy expertise and constituent feedback is actively disabling their own legislative capacity. The district's human capital is its greatest asset for governance, and treating these constituencies as passive voters rather than active partners in policy development represents a failure of representative function." }
