          { name: "Renter Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 36.5% of SC-HD-82 renters and median household income of $46,220, housing cost burden is a direct material constraint on one-third of the district's households. Renters cannot build equity, have zero shelter from inflation, and are economically vulnerable to wage stagnation in ways homeowners are not. Any representative claiming to serve this district must deliver on concrete renter protections — tenant rights enforcement, affordable housing development partnerships with local nonprofits, rental assistance programs — with visible results that renters can directly experience in their monthly budgets." },
          { name: "Wage Growth & Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 17.6% college-attainment rate is the lowest meaningful educational credential rate in the district, reflecting a constituency without credential-based career pathways into economic mobility. At $46,220 median income in a high-cost state, wage stagnation is a direct economic problem — not a future risk. Focus on job training, apprenticeship programs, and direct wage support mechanisms that connect available workers to available jobs at livable wages, rather than abstract human capital rhetoric that assumes education credentials are accessible to everyone." },
          { name: "Black Voter Consolidation & Persuasion", tag: "careful", why: "The 54-46 Democratic margin in a district with 48.78% Black population is narrower than baseline Black voting patterns in South Carolina would predict; this suggests either meaningful Republican persuasion among Black voters or Democratic turnout vulnerability among this demographic. The strategic question is whether Black voters in SC-HD-82 are evaluating on different priorities than assumed, or whether they are simply not being reached with messages or constituent services that convince them that voting Democratic materially improves their lives. Conducting constituent listening directly with Black voters who are not voting Democratic — and with Black voters who are voting Democratic but at lower rates than expected — is essential to understanding whether the margin is stable or at risk." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Stable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where median income is $46,220, 36.5% of households are renters, and college attainment is 17.6%, a 54-46 margin is not a comfortable Democratic hold — it is a plurality in a genuinely competitive district where voters are evaluating on material outcomes. Any candidate or representative who treats 54% as a safe majority and consolidates the existing coalition without actively persuading opposition voters or delivering visible results for renters and low-income constituents will find the margin vulnerable in a challenging election cycle." },
          { name: "Conflating Black Majority Status with Automatic Democratic Support", tag: "avoid", why: "Majority-Black districts do not automatically produce large Democratic margins — the actual margin depends on whether the representative is delivering on the material concerns of Black voters in that specific district. SC-HD-82's 54-46 result suggests that some portion of Black voters are either not persuaded that voting Democratic improves their circumstances, or are voting but at lower turnout rates than expected. Assuming Black majority status equals electoral safety — rather than engaging Black voters directly on why they are voting as they are — is a strategic error that will manifest as lost votes in the next competitive cycle." }
