          { name: "Racial & Ethnic Divisiveness", tag: "careful", why: "The district is 65.43% White with significant Black (22.21%) and Hispanic (7.72%) populations, totaling roughly 30% combined non-White residents. Messaging must acknowledge all communities' distinct concerns—crime, economic opportunity, education quality, and local investment—without resorting to zero-sum framing. The unopposed 2024 victory suggests no recent partisan pressure, but demographic diversity means future challengers may organize around specific community grievances if current representation appears indifferent to minority concerns." },
          { name: "Immigration & Border Rhetoric", tag: "avoid", why: "While Hispanic residents represent 7.72% of the district, state-level immigration rhetoric often alienates Latino voters without moving the needle on local economic drivers. The district's real challenges—household income, housing affordability, job quality—are not meaningfully shaped by immigration policy. Inflammatory rhetoric risks poisoning relationships with a growing demographic segment without gaining ground on issues constituents actually vote on." },
          { name: "Culture War & Social Grievance Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "The district's median age of 38.3 and moderate college-education rate (22.4%) suggest a pragmatic, working-family electorate focused on immediate material concerns rather than national cultural flashpoints. Unopposed incumbency and lack of recent partisan turbulence indicate low appetite for culture-war messaging. Resources are better spent on kitchen-table issues—property values, job markets, school funding—that move votes in this constituency." }
