          { name: "Minority Community Base Maintenance", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 25.5% Black and 8.8% Hispanic populations together constitute approximately one-third of the district and are the most reliable Democratic constituency in SC-HD-86. The unopposed victory in 2024 means these communities have not been tested by a credible Republican challenger in recent cycles, and Democratic strength among them cannot be assumed indefinitely without active constituent service, visible community presence, and legislative victories on issues like healthcare access and economic mobility that matter to these voters materially." },
          { name: "Organizational Strength Behind Unopposed Status", tag: "careful", why: "An unopposed victory is a positive electoral outcome but can mask organizational weakness, declining constituent engagement, or false assumptions about the durability of Democratic strength. SC-HD-86's 100% result in 2024 does not reveal whether the incumbent has built durable local organization, maintains regular constituent contact, or has invested in infrastructure that can respond quickly if a Republican challenger emerges with economic anxiety messaging targeted at the 40.5-year-old white homeowner plurality. The absence of opposition is a tactical advantage in the short term but strategically fragile if organizational investment has declined." },
          { name: "Assuming College-Education Gap as a Democratic Liability", tag: "avoid", why: "The 20.4% college-education rate is lower than national Democratic strongholds, but this does not make SC-HD-86 inherently Republican or hostile to Democratic governance. Working-class and non-college-educated voters can vote Democratic when they perceive government as responsive to their material economic concerns and when Democratic candidates and legislators actively engage them on those concerns. Assuming the education gap is a structural Democratic weakness rather than an opportunity to build blue-collar economic governance messaging is a strategic error." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Victory as Permanent Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "The 100% result in 2024 is genuine but historically temporary—uncontested seats often attract challengers in the next cycle, particularly if the incumbent becomes perceived as complacent or distant from constituent concerns. A Republican candidate who makes healthcare costs, property tax fairness, and government responsiveness to working families the central campaign theme will find an audience among the 40.5-year-old white homeowner plurality, unopposed status notwithstanding. Treating this seat as permanently safe rather than as a district requiring active constituent engagement and organizational investment is a path to losing a seat that should be defensible." }
