          "The strategic vulnerability here is not partisan—it is one of representation depth. Running unopposed in a district this economically stressed creates permission for an incumbent to govern without tight constituent feedback, and that permission is dangerous in a seat where 23.9% of households are renters (above-average vulnerability to economic shocks), median age is 41.1 (a working-age constituency with direct stakes in employment, healthcare, and cost of living), and the educational attainment gap suggests limited access to the economic mobility pathways that typically anchor working-class political loyalty. An opponent who emerges in 2026 or 2028 and offers a specific, credible narrative about job creation, cost-of-living relief, or educational access in a district where these are genuine constraints could exploit the organizational and messaging gaps that unopposed incumbency creates. The 100% result should be read not as a durable coalition but as a baseline that requires active reinforcement through visible, specific governance on the issues that matter most to the 55.6% Black majority and the working-class white and Hispanic minorities."
