          { name: "Partisan Ideology Framing Over Material Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "SC-HD-100's unopposed Democratic result should not invite positioning around abstract conservative-versus-liberal ideology or inherited partisan loyalty; this constituency evaluates based on whether government is delivering on concrete economic security concerns — healthcare costs, property taxes, school quality. A legislator who campaigns or governs primarily through partisan ideology frames rather than material governance solutions will find that framing hollow and unconvincing to voters who elected them for practical results." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Victory as a Durable Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "The strategic error in SC-HD-100 is assuming that an unopposed 2024 result guarantees a safe seat in the next cycle. Unopposed victories reflect absence of opposition, not absence of persuadable voters or governance grievances that a future challenger could organize around. A representative who uses an unopposed result as permission to reduce constituent engagement, neglect specific economic concerns, or govern primarily for a partisan base rather than for the material security of the mixed-race, middle-class, homeowner-majority community will create the conditions for a competitive race in the next cycle." }
