        memoHeadline: "SC-HD-104: A Deeply Aging Homeowner District at Risk of Invisibility — 59.5-Year Median Age, 81.4% Homeowners, and the Electoral Danger of an Unopposed Victory.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "SC-HD-104 is structurally Republican — 78.4% white, 81.4% homeowners, $64,128 median income, and an unopposed 2024 result — but its defining demographic characteristic is extreme age. At 59.5 years median age, this is the oldest constituency in the reference sequence by a substantial margin, and that age profile is the lens through which all other district characteristics must be understood. The 81.4% homeownership rate reflects not just Republican lean but a cohort of older adults whose economic security depends on stable property values, predictable tax burdens, and reliable access to healthcare. The $64,128 median income is middle-class but offers limited buffer for unexpected costs. For a constituency at this age and income, the representative's responsiveness to immediate material concerns — healthcare affordability, rural service viability, property tax fairness — directly determines whether voters perceive their legislator as serving them or attending to other priorities.",
          "The unopposed 2024 victory should not be mistaken for electoral security; it is more likely a reflection of candidate recruitment and funding constraints in a Republican-lean rural district than evidence of genuine constituent consolidation. The 18.6% renter rate and 29.4% college attainment, while smaller segments than the homeowner majority, are still meaningful populations whose concerns — housing affordability, education funding, economic opportunity — may not yet be mobilized into opposition but are vulnerable to activation by a credible challenger. The district's extreme age profile means that healthcare access, Medicare policy, rural hospital viability, and prescription drug affordability are not peripheral issues — they are the primary constituent concerns that will determine whether Bailey retains district confidence. A legislator perceived as responsive to aging homeowners' economic security concerns has secured this seat; one perceived as inattentive to them has handed an opening to whoever can demonstrate they take those concerns seriously."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with aging homeowner economic security: healthcare affordability, rural hospital access, property tax fairness, and Medicare sustainability are the material concerns of a 59.5-year-old, 81.4% homeowner constituency at $64,128 income — and the primary factors determining constituent satisfaction.",
          "Monitor and engage the 18.6% renter and 29.4% college-educated segments actively; unopposed victory creates a temptation to neglect these groups, but either segment mobilized by a credible challenger on housing affordability or education funding becomes an electoral liability.",
          "Engage rural healthcare infrastructure with specificity: hospital accessibility, emergency care viability, and rural clinic sustainability are not abstract policy questions but direct constituent concerns in a district with this age profile and geography.",
          "Avoid the political logic of the unopposed race: the absence of 2024 opposition reflects district lean, not constituent consensus; the next competitive cycle will test whether constituent loyalty to Bailey is rooted in active service or passive Republican default."
        ]
