        memoHeadline: "SC-HD-109: A 49% Black, 45% Renter District Won Unopposed—Strategic Stability Masks Fragility in a Working-Class, Education-Constrained Constituency.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "SC-HD-109 is a working-class, majority-Black district with median household income of $62,125, college attainment of only 21.3%, and a 45.3% renter rate—demographic signals of a constituency with significant economic constraint and limited educational credential accumulation. The district's racial composition (49.19% Black, 32.42% white, 13.49% Hispanic, 2.42% Asian, with ~2.5% AIAN) reflects a genuinely diverse working-class community rather than a simple majority-minority district. The unopposed 2024 victory for Spann-Wilder suggests either strong incumbent consolidation of the district's Black voter base or an absence of credible Republican opposition—but unopposed victories, while electorally comfortable in the moment, often mask underlying constituency concerns that can surface when a challenger emerges. At $62,125 median income with 45% renting, this is a district where housing costs, wage stagnation, and access to affordable childcare are material concerns for most households; a representative who is not actively engaged in addressing those economic pressures is vulnerable to a challenger who can frame them as neglected.",
          "The strategic vulnerability of SC-HD-109 lies in the conjunction of low college attainment (21.3%), a high renter rate (45.3%), and the absence of a meaningful electoral contest in 2024. Low educational attainment in a district this economically constrained often correlates with limited awareness of incumbent accomplishments or specific legislative record—voters in such constituencies often evaluate on whether they perceive government to be improving their material conditions, not on abstract policy positions. The 45% renter rate is particularly significant: renters in South Carolina have fewer property-tax-based relationships to government and often experience housing insecurity more acutely than homeowners, making housing affordability, landlord regulation, and neighborhood investment direct pocketbook concerns. An unopposed victory prevents the incumbent from building the narrative infrastructure—specific legislative wins, community presence, constituent service operations—that makes re-election in 2026 or beyond more durable than the current margin suggests. Spann-Wilder should treat the unopposed status as an opportunity to build that infrastructure now, not as evidence that the seat is secure absent active constituent engagement."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Treat unopposed 2024 status as a vulnerability signal, not a security blanket: without a meaningful electoral contest, the incumbent has not had to articulate a specific legislative record or constituent-service narrative that resonates with a working-class, 45% renter constituency—building that narrative now prevents a credible 2026 challenger from defining the seat as neglectful.",
          "Lead with housing affordability and renter protections: at 45.3% renter rate and $62,125 median income, housing cost is the primary constraint on household economic security; specific legislative proposals on tenant rights, landlord regulation, affordable housing development, and rental assistance are the most direct way to demonstrate responsiveness to the district's material concerns.",
          "Address childcare access and workforce participation explicitly: with high renter rates and constrained household incomes, many SC-HD-109 families face childcare costs that exceed housing costs as a percentage of income; state policy on childcare subsidies, pre-K access, and flexible work standards directly affects whether two-income households can achieve economic stability.",
          "Invest in constituent service and local presence despite unopposed status: knock on doors, hold office hours, and build relationships with neighborhood organizations in the 49% Black, 13.5% Hispanic communities—an unopposed victory is precisely when an incumbent should be building the visible, tangible constituent relationship that survives the inevitable emergence of future opposition."
        ]
