          { name: "Partisan Attack Messaging in an Educated Suburb", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated voters at 67.4% attainment are more resistant to partisan attack messaging and more responsive to issue-specific critique grounded in policy substance — abstract attacks on 'the other side' land weaker with this constituency than specific critiques of opponent positions on healthcare, taxation, or education. Campaigns that lean heavily on partisan conflict messaging rather than demonstrating their own competence on constituent priorities tend to underperform with affluent, educated suburban voters, who often perceive such messaging as evasion of real governance questions." },
          { name: "Taking the 56% Result as a Safe Foundation", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 result in a district with 67.4% college attainment and $102,897 income is not a comfortable majority — it reflects a constituency actively evaluating alternatives and willing to vote based on candidate quality rather than party identity. Any incumbent who campaigns defensively, assumes the 56% is secure, or focuses energy on base mobilization rather than persuading the educated center is arithmetically miscalculating; the 44% opposition in an affluent, educated district is more sophisticated, more likely to shift with candidate quality, and more likely to organize against a complacent incumbent than partisan bases in less educated districts." }
