          "Build explicit, visible engagement with the Black professional-class community: the 29% Black population represents approximately 25,000-28,000 voters whose income, education, and homeownership profiles likely mirror the district average — this is an established community, not a low-income constituency, and requires ongoing constituent service, visible representation in campaign and legislative activity, and specific responsiveness to community concerns.",
          "Address college-educated swing voters' actual governance concerns: healthcare cost containment, property tax fairness, infrastructure investment, and whether government is competent and responsive matter more to $85K households with college education than partisan positioning — incumbent or challenger must demonstrate concrete competence on these issues to consolidate this group.",
          "Treat 51-49 as the unstable equilibrium it is: any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though a one-point margin in a college-educated, 29% Black suburban district is a stable base is arithmetically wrong — the seat will belong to whichever candidate more effectively serves and engages both the educated white suburban constituency and the Black professional community."
