          { name: "Homeowner Majority Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a 70.1% homeownership rate and median income of $92,586, SC-HD-119 is a homeowner-dominant district where property values, property tax structure, school funding quality, and housing affordability are direct voter concerns. The 55% Democratic margin suggests the homeowner majority is not reflexively Republican in this district — a signal that they are evaluating on competence and specific policy delivery rather than partisan identity. For the incumbent, this means property tax fairness, neighborhood investment, and school quality are non-negotiable governance priorities; for any challenger, it means the homeowner majority is persuadable on the basis of economic stewardship." },
          { name: "Black Community Constituent Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 17.13% of the district population, the Black community represents approximately one in six constituents — a meaningful minority community whose voter participation and candidate preference directly affect a 55-45 margin. In South Carolina suburban contexts, Black voter turnout and engagement patterns have demonstrated significant variance across election cycles, and the specific policy issues that matter to this community — criminal justice, education equity, economic opportunity, and healthcare access — require active constituent attention rather than assumption of loyalty. The 55% margin is narrow enough that shifts in Black voter participation or enthusiasm could materially affect the outcome." },
          { name: "Treating 55% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55-45 result in any district is not a comfortable majority — it is a 10-point margin in a constituency that is actively divided and capable of shifting with changed circumstances or a credible alternative. In a college-educated, affluent suburban district like SC-HD-119, swing voters evaluate on competence and governance outcomes rather than partisan loyalty, which means any incumbent who campaigns as though 55% is a durable floor rather than a contested coalition is vulnerable to a challenger who offers a compelling alternative vision. The 29.9% renter rate — substantially above the homeowner majority — also represents a constituent base whose housing and economic concerns may not be fully addressed by homeowner-focused governance." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging Without Constituent Service Substance", tag: "avoid", why: "SC-HD-119's 51.8% college attainment and $92,586 income profile indicate a constituency that evaluates candidates on competence and policy specificity rather than partisan theater. A representative or challenger who relies primarily on partisan messaging without demonstrating mastery of the actual governance issues that matter to affluent suburban voters — school funding mechanisms, infrastructure investment, healthcare cost containment, tax policy — will be perceived as inauthentic and unserious by a constituency that has the educational and informational resources to distinguish rhetoric from substance. In this district, credibility is built on demonstrated competence, not partisan affiliation." }
