          "The strategic vulnerability in SC-HD-120 is not that the Republican margin is fragile in demographic terms — it is not — but that a 67% result can be eroded by an incumbent who campaigns as though the margin is permanent rather than something that requires active maintenance. In affluent, college-educated suburban districts, swing voters exist and vote: educated professionals and homeowners who evaluate primarily on governance competence, fiscal discipline, and whether local institutions are functioning effectively. A challenger who reframes the race around specific governance failures — underfunded schools, property tax burden, or fiscal mismanagement — can move educated swing voters even in an otherwise safely Republican district. The incumbent must lead with concrete demonstrations of fiscal stewardship, constituent service responsiveness, and protection of the economic interests that define this constituency's primary concerns. Without that active maintenance, the 33% opposition becomes less a minority position and more a plausible alternative."
