          { name: "Pursuing Ideological Conflict or Cultural Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "A 70-30 Republican district held by consistent performance on routine governance concerns has no strategic incentive to organize the electorate around cultural or ideological conflict. Such positioning risks activating the Democratic 30% around those issues while fracturing the homeowner base on local governance failures. The 70% result is earned through competence, not secured through cultural alignment; treating the seat as a platform for ideological messaging squanders the implicit mandate for routine effective administration." },
          { name: "Taking the Homeowner Base for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "A 70% margin is durable only through continuous demonstration of competence on property taxes, schools, and infrastructure — the issues that drive the homeowner majority's satisfaction. The moment the incumbent becomes visible primarily through state-level partisan activity or becomes associated with local governance failure, the homeowner base's loyalty becomes contingent on whether a credible alternative emerges. A 70% result is not permission to reduce constituent attention or assume no challenger will successfully compete for the base; it is evidence that routine competence and visibility hold the district, and neglecting either invites vulnerability." }
