          { name: "Black Constituency Engagement & Healthcare Disparities", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 24.98% of the district population, the Black community represents roughly one-quarter of constituents and faces measurable disparities in healthcare access, employment opportunity, and economic mobility compared to the white majority. The 70% Republican margin may indicate current partisan alignment among some Black voters, but it does not indicate settled satisfaction with representation—particularly around healthcare quality, maternal mortality, workforce development, and criminal justice. A legislator who demonstrates visible, ongoing constituent service to the Black community and offers concrete policy solutions on healthcare access and economic opportunity creates a relationship that survives partisan pressure and becomes harder for opposition to move. Invisibility or generic outreach invites an opposition candidate to mobilize this population as a swing constituency." },
          { name: "Unmapped Demographic Constituents (4.4% Gap)", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 95.6% of SC-SD-27's population, leaving 4.4% (roughly 3,500-4,000 constituents) whose identity, needs, and political alignment remain unknown—likely including American Indian residents, mixed-race households, and other communities whose concerns may be distinct from the majority populations. In a district this size, 4.4% is not trivial, and unmapped constituents can become either invisible in representation or mobilized as a swing constituency by opposition if they perceive neglect. The incumbent or challenger who invests in direct neighborhood-level constituent contact to understand and engage this gap gains both a governance advantage and an electoral shield against the perception that representation is incomplete." },
          { name: "Treating 70% as a Durable Safe Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "A 70% result in a working-class district with $56,286 median income, limited college attainment, and significant Black population is not a signal of settled voter satisfaction or partisan loyalty—it is a margin contingent on visible delivery of economic results and demonstrated constituent engagement. In districts with limited economic mobility and rising cost-of-living pressure, voter alignment is transactional: voters reward the party or candidate offering material delivery on jobs, healthcare, and housing costs. Any incumbent who assumes the 70% is durable and does not actively demonstrate ongoing economic delivery or visible constituent service to the Black community and economically-pressed working-class majority risks finding the margin weaker than it appears when opposition runs a credible alternative on cost-of-living or healthcare accessibility." },
          { name: "Framing the District as Homogeneous or Settled", tag: "avoid", why: "SC-SD-27 contains meaningful demographic complexity—a 24.98% Black population, 5.34% Hispanic community, 4.4% unmapped constituents, and a white working-class majority that is economically diverse (some homeowners with assets, many renters under cost pressure)—that can be flattened into a single partisan narrative only at the cost of losing visibility into actual constituent needs and potential swing constituencies. Any candidate or incumbent who frames the district as simply 'red' or 'Republican' without engaging the specific economic concerns of working-class voters, the distinct healthcare and opportunity challenges of the Black community, or the needs of the unmapped 4.4% is governing a fictional district, not the actual one, and leaving an opening for opposition to mobilize overlooked constituencies." }
