          { name: "Property Tax & Retirement Security Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 82.3% homeownership and a median age of 52.8, the district is composed overwhelmingly of people whose primary wealth is their home and whose primary concern is protecting that asset and their retirement income. Property tax assessment practices, exemptions for seniors, and tax stability are not abstract policy debates in SC-SD-28—they are direct pocket-book issues for constituents evaluating their financial security. A representative who demonstrates fluency on property tax policy, engages local assessors and county commissions on equity, and can explain specific protections for older homeowners commands the credibility this demographic demands." },
          { name: "Invisible Minority Communities & Service Equity", tag: "careful", why: "The 4.4% demographic gap in SC-SD-28 represents residents whose presence is not captured in the reported Census categories—likely American Indian, multi-racial, or other minority populations. At 4.4%, this population is small enough that it can become entirely invisible in a district dominated by affluent white homeowners, yet large enough that neglect creates perception of exclusion. In an unopposed environment, baseline constituent service accessibility for these populations—language services, economic development engagement, healthcare access—becomes both a governance obligation and a latent electoral vulnerability if a challenger ever emerges and mobilizes them." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Electoral Security", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 100% victory is not a measure of durable political strength—it reflects an absence of organized opposition in this particular cycle. Unopposed victories often mask underlying economic anxieties or dissatisfaction that have not yet crystallized into a credible challenger. In a district of affluent older homeowners, rapid economic deterioration (recession, healthcare crisis, property tax shock) can shift voter sentiment from contentment to active dissatisfaction within a single election cycle, and an incumbent who has treated 100% as permission to disengage will be vulnerable to a well-positioned challenger who addresses that dissatisfaction." },
          { name: "Assuming Partisan Stability in an Aging, Asset-Holding Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "SC-SD-28's demographic profile—affluent, older, Anglo, homeowner-heavy—creates an appearance of Republican stability that should not be confused with constituent satisfaction. Older homeowners who perceive threats to retirement security, healthcare access, or property values can shift their voting behavior with remarkable speed when they conclude their material interests are under threat. Generic Republican positioning that does not specifically address the economic concerns of a 52.8-year-old homeowner constituency misses the actual basis of voter alignment in this district. This is a population that votes on economic security, not ideology—and that distinction shapes how a representative must actually serve them." }
