        issues: [
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security and Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $43,038 median household income with a 31.7% renter rate, SC-SD-29 is a working-class constituency for whom housing costs consume a disproportionate share of household budgets. In a district split 50-50 between Black and white voters, both communities experience similar material pressures: whether they can afford rent, whether eviction is a realistic threat, whether wages are keeping pace with inflation. Specific legislative action on affordable housing development, tenant protections, and rent stabilization is both a policy domain where state government has direct authority and an issue that cuts across racial lines. A candidate who offers concrete answers—not platitudes—on housing affordability will find receptive audiences in both the Black and white working-class portions of the district." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Medicaid Expansion in a Low-Income District", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a median household income of $43,038 and college attainment at 16.3%, SC-SD-29 has a high proportion of households without employer-sponsored healthcare and limited capacity to absorb medical expenses. Whether Medicaid expansion is actively available, whether rural clinics in the district remain open, and whether prescription drug costs are manageable are not abstract healthcare policy questions—they are direct questions about whether working-class families can access care without financial catastrophe. A candidate who takes a clear, specific position on Medicaid expansion and rural healthcare infrastructure, tied to the material reality of working-class household budgets, will gain traction with voters evaluating on economic survival rather than partisan identity." },
          { name: "Black Voter Coalition Building and Community-Specific Economic Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "Black voters comprise 46.64% of SC-SD-29 and are not a secondary constituency to be managed after consolidating white support—they are necessary to any winning coalition in a 50-50 district. Educational equity, workforce development programs that reach Black workers, and economic development investment in Black neighborhoods are simultaneously moral imperatives and smart electoral strategy. A candidate who engages the Black community with substantive policy commitments on schools, jobs, and entrepreneurship—not symbolic gestures—will build authentic political power; a candidate who treats Black voters as a demographic box to check will generate opposition that turns a 50-50 race into a loss." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideological Framing Over Constituent Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district split exactly 50-50 between parties and 46-46 between Black and white voters, partisan ideological positioning ('freedom vs. socialism,' etc.) is a poor substitute for concrete answers to economic pressure. Working-class voters of both races in SC-SD-29 are evaluating based on whether their material situation is improving, not whether the candidate's rhetoric aligns with their inherited partisan loyalty. A candidate who defaults to abstract conservative or progressive framing without tying it to specific household-level economic outcomes will find that framing irrelevant to a constituency evaluating on survival and opportunity." },
          { name: "Assuming the Incumbent's 50% Is a Durable Base", tag: "avoid", why: "Chaplin's 50% victory was mathematically a tie with an exactly equal opposition—and in a district this closely divided, 50% is not a floor but a precarious plurality held in place by specific decisions and community relationships. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 50% represents a safe majority and relies on consolidating existing Republican voters while neglecting working-class concerns will find the seat at genuine risk in the next cycle. This is a district where active constituent engagement and tangible delivery on economic concerns is the only sustainable electoral strategy." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "SC-SD-29: A Perfectly Divided District Where Black-White Parity and Razor Margins Demand Specific Constituent Delivery on Economic Security",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "SC-SD-29 is a district of near-perfect demographic and electoral balance—46.64% Black, 46.02% white, 50% Democratic, 50% Republican—that produces a fundamentally different strategic reality than a district with a comfortable incumbent margin. Chaplin's 50% win in 2024 was not a mandate; it was a plurality of contested voters in a constituency where roughly half the electorate actively chose someone else. The district's economic profile—$43,038 median household income, 16.3% college attainment, 31.7% renter rate—signals a working-class constituency under material financial pressure. For a district this evenly divided, the difference between holding a seat and losing it is not ideology or partisan branding; it is concrete delivery on the economic and governance concerns that working-class households in SC-SD-29 experience daily: wage stagnation, healthcare access, housing affordability, and whether government investment is reaching their community.",
          "The strategic vulnerability in a 50-50 split is that neither the incumbent nor a challenger can assume a stable base. Black voters in SC-SD-29 represent nearly half the district and have demonstrated the capacity to swing elections when they conclude a candidate is not delivering for their community; white voters at 46% are similarly swing-prone in a district where economic anxiety rather than racial or ideological loyalty appears to be the primary voting driver. The 31.7% renter rate—significantly above the state average—indicates a constituency with limited housing wealth and high sensitivity to eviction risk, utility costs, and whether state policy is helping or hurting their ability to afford shelter. With a median age of 41.7 and college attainment at 16.3%, this is a constituency without the asset buffers or educational credentials that cushion economic volatility. Any candidate who wants to hold or win SC-SD-29 must offer a specific answer to why working-class households—regardless of race—should believe their economic situation will improve under their representation, and that answer must be grounded in tangible policy and community investment, not partisan rhetoric."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Treat 50% as a tied race, not a floor: the incumbent has no margin for error and must actively defend by demonstrating concrete delivery to working-class households on housing affordability, healthcare access, and wage-supporting economic development—not by consolidating a base and assuming electoral safety.",
          "Lead with economic security for renters and working-class homeowners: at $43,038 income and 31.7% renter rate, SC-SD-29 is a constituency for whom housing costs, utility bills, and healthcare expense are material household budget concerns; specific policy solutions on affordable housing, tenant protections, and healthcare expansion will resonate across both Black and white working-class voters.",
          "Engage the Black community with substantive policy, not symbolic acknowledgment: Black voters represent 46.64% of the district and are not a monolithic bloc—they are teachers, workers, small business owners, and renters with specific economic concerns; a candidate who addresses police accountability, educational investment, and economic opportunity with the same specificity they bring to white working-class concerns will build a durable coalition.",
          "Monitor and respond to the 31.7% renter constituency as a distinct strategic group: renters in working-class districts are often mobilized around eviction prevention, rent stabilization, and habitability standards; this is both a policy area where legislative action is visible and a demographic that can swing a 50-50 race if they believe a candidate is fighting for their housing security."
        ]
