        issues: [
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security and Wage Stagnation", tag: "lean-into", why: "SC-SD-40's $46,332 median household income and 27.6% renter rate define a constituency living on constrained margins where healthcare costs, childcare affordability, housing stability, and wage growth are not abstract policy concerns but immediate material pressures. The 18.2% college attainment rate signals a working-class labor force competing in a market that has not generated wage increases proportional to cost-of-living growth. A candidate who speaks with specificity about job quality, manufacturing, small-business support, and the cost of living — not in generic terms but grounded in the real expenses of SC-SD-40 households — builds the credibility necessary to consolidate the 53% or flip the 47%." },
          { name: "Black Community Engagement and Accountability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 48.87% Black, this constituency is the demographic base of any Democratic hold, and the tightness of Hutto's 53% win suggests real questions about whether the incumbent is delivering on education quality, public safety, economic opportunity, and healthcare access — the issues that Black voters in South Carolina evaluate on. Authentic community engagement, transparent communication about governance challenges and priorities, and visible responsiveness to constituent service create the foundation of a coalition that is motivated, not merely inherited. Any candidate — incumbent or challenger — who takes the Black vote for granted while focusing messaging elsewhere will find that 53% erodes quickly." },
          { name: "White Working-Class Persuadability in a 53-47 Seat", tag: "careful", why: "The white 43.1% of SC-SD-40 is not monolithic: some portion votes Democratic on economic or social grounds, while some portion is persuadable depending on how candidates frame job creation, trade, and working-class economic policy. In a seat this close, the conventional assumption that white working-class voters are irretrievable is both analytically false and strategically dangerous. A candidate who can credibly argue that their economic agenda — whether manufacturing support, union protections, or trade policy — delivers for working people has a direct path to persuading swing voters in this community." },
          { name: "Treating 53% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 result is a narrow plurality, not a secure majority, in a district where 47% of voters actively chose someone else. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though it is safely held, or any challenger who approaches it as unwinnable, is misreading both the arithmetic and the underlying dynamics. The combination of working-class economic stress, a closely divided racial demographics (48.87% Black, 43.1% white), and low college attainment means that voter preference can shift quickly if a candidate's economic message or constituent responsiveness falters." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging Detached from Material Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where median income is $46,332 and renters comprise 27.6% of households, abstract partisan rhetoric about governance philosophy or national political identity is a weaker organizing principle than concrete policy answers to healthcare costs, wage stagnation, and housing affordability. A candidate who leads with partisan affiliation rather than specific economic proposals — or who assumes that party label is sufficient to consolidate a coalition — is ceding persuadable voters to an opponent with a more credible economic narrative. The 47% opposition exists in part because messaging has not matched material concerns." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "SC-SD-40: A Narrow 53-47 Democratic Hold in a Working-Class, Majority-Black District Where Economic Security and Authentic Community Engagement Determine Durability",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "SC-SD-40 is a genuinely competitive district that Hutto won with only 53% in 2024 — a margin that, while technically a majority, reveals a constituency deeply divided and actively contested. The district's demographic profile explains why: it is 48.87% Black and 43.1% white, with a median household income of $46,332 and a renter rate of 27.6%, making it a working-class community where both homeowners and renters are living on constrained margins. The college-educated rate of 18.2% and median age of 41.4 indicate a mature, non-college-credentialed workforce competing in a labor market that has not generated wage growth proportional to cost-of-living increases. The 47% opposition — nearly half the district — did not turn out against Hutto because of abstract partisan messaging; they voted against him because of concrete economic anxiety, skepticism about whether government is delivering for working people, or failure to perceive authentic engagement from the incumbent. In a district this close, with this demographic profile, the 53% is not a foundation — it is a vulnerable plurality that will shift if the challenger offers a more credible message about economic security.",
          "The racial composition of SC-SD-40 — split between a Black majority at 48.87% and a white plurality at 43.1%, with smaller Hispanic (4.71%), Asian (0.9%), and unaccounted-for populations — creates a governing and electoral obligation that Hutto's 53% margin suggests he may not be fully meeting. Black voters in South Carolina have demonstrated both loyalty to Democratic candidates and a capacity to withhold support or split their vote when they perceive that a representative is not delivering on economic security, public safety, education quality, or healthcare access. The white 43.1% of the district is not monolithic: some portion votes Democratic based on economic interests or social-justice commitments, while some portion votes Republican based on cultural or fiscal conservatism. The 53-47 result indicates that Hutto consolidated enough of the Black vote and captured enough white voters to win, but the tightness of the margin suggests that neither coalition is secure. A challenger who can speak credibly to working-class economic concerns — job quality, healthcare costs, childcare affordability, housing stability — while building authentic relationships with both the Black community and white working-class voters has a direct path to flipping this seat. Hutto must understand that his 53% is not a safe hold; it is a ceiling that turns into a floor only if he actively addresses the material concerns of voters who came close to rejecting him."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with working-class economic security: at $46,332 median income with 27.6% renters and 18.2% college attainment, SC-SD-40 voters evaluate on whether wages are rising, healthcare is affordable, childcare supports families, and housing is stable — not on partisan abstractions. Any candidate must offer specific policy responses to these material concerns.",
          "Engage the Black community with authenticity and accountability: 48.87% Black voters are the demographic base of any Democratic hold, but the 47% opposition suggests real skepticism about whether the incumbent is delivering. Town halls, constituent service responsiveness, and transparent communication about what government can and cannot accomplish are the foundation of a durable Black coalition.",
          "Do not concede the white working-class vote: at 43.1%, white voters are a plurality, and a portion of them are persuadable on economic grounds if a candidate is credible about job creation, trade policy, and manufacturing revival — issues that cut across partisan lines in working-class communities. Assuming they are irretrievable is a strategic error in a 53-47 seat.",
          "Treat 53% as a vulnerable plurality, not a secure majority: with 47% opposition in a district this economically stressed, any incumbent must be actively engaged with constituent concerns, responsive to service requests, and transparent about governance choices. A 53% win is an instruction to listen harder and deliver more visibly, not permission to consolidate and hold."
        ]
