          { name: "Treating 57% as a Safe Republican Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57-43 result in a college-educated, $84k+ median income suburban district is not a safe Republican margin—it is a plurality in a constituency of swing voters capable of supporting either party. The 43% opposition is not a floor but represents affluent voters actively choosing Democrats; any candidate who assumes this coalition is stable without continuously earning swing-voter support through competence and policy delivery will find the margin collapse in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Rural or Agricultural Constituency Framing", tag: "avoid", why: "SC-SD-41 is a suburban district with a median age of 38.8, median income of $84,831, and only a 23.8% renter rate—this is a homeowner-professional community, not a rural agricultural constituency. Any candidate who defaults to rural economic messaging, agricultural policy, or small-town nostalgia will miss the actual electorate's material concerns around education quality, healthcare costs, and whether government is delivering value to suburban professionals and business owners. This constituency's issues are suburban issues, not rural ones." }
