          { name: "Renter Constituency Mobilization & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 32.4% renter rate represents approximately one-third of the district—a meaningful minority fundamentally different from the homeowner majority in economic vulnerability, mobility, and policy priorities. Renters in affluent districts are often younger, more mobile, and more exposed to housing cost volatility; they are the only demographic segment in SC-SD-44 where Democratic messaging on rent stabilization, housing affordability, and tenant protections has material resonance. Any Democrat attempting to compete in this district must mobilize renter turnout aggressively and reframe the economic narrative around housing cost pressure as a material constraint on younger households' economic security." },
          { name: "Maintaining Affluent-Suburb Coalition Stability", tag: "careful", why: "The 61-39 Republican result reflects genuine ideological and economic alignment between the district's college-educated homeowner majority and Republican fiscal and education policy—but this coalition is not automatically permanent if economic conditions shift or if a credible challenger reframes the policy debate. The 42.6% college attainment rate means this constituency evaluates on substance; if education funding deteriorates, healthcare costs rise disproportionately, or school quality declines, the incumbent cannot simply rely on partisan identity to hold the margin. The 61% is durable only if the Republican candidate continues delivering measurable results on the issues affluent professionals actually care about." },
          { name: "Framing Renter Concerns as 'Special Interest' Rather Than Constituent Reality", tag: "avoid", why: "With 32.4% renters, framing housing affordability, tenant protections, or rent stabilization as 'special interest' positions rather than constituency concerns is strategically self-defeating—it implicitly dismisses one-third of the district's actual residents. A candidate who dismisses renter concerns as peripheral to the 'real' district (homeowners) is ceding the renter vote unopposed and creating an opening for a challenger to present themselves as genuinely representative of the full constituency." },
          { name: "Treating the 61% as Untouchable or Running an Incumbent Protection Campaign Without Substance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61% result in a district this affluent and educated is durable only if the incumbent can point to measurable achievements on public goods that matter to college-educated professionals: school quality, infrastructure investment, healthcare access, fiscal competence. Running a campaign that assumes the 61% is a structural floor without delivering substantive results on these dimensions invites a credible challenger to reframe the race around whether the current representation is actually serving the district's material interests. The college-educated homeowner majority will abandon an incumbent if persuaded that an alternative would better deliver on their priorities." }
