          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security & Cost-of-Living Relief", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $53,756 median household income with 28.6% renter rate and only 21.4% college attainment, SC-SD-45 is a working-class district where healthcare costs, housing affordability, and job quality directly affect household stability. Unlike districts with higher incomes or asset accumulation, this constituency cannot absorb economic shocks—which means that messaging on cost-of-living support, small business job creation, and healthcare access is not a secondary issue but a primary electoral driver. A legislator who addresses these issues with specificity and can show tangible constituent service results on economic mobility is building the durable foundation that makes the seat defensible against future challenge." },
          { name: "Cross-Racial Working-Class Constituency Building", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 43% Black, 42% white, 10% Hispanic composition means no single racial group is a supermajority—and the unopposed victory should not obscure the arithmetic reality that white and Hispanic working-class voters are necessary to defend a 55%+ margin against organized opposition. Economic messaging that speaks to all three communities' concerns about jobs, housing, and healthcare is more strategically durable than appeals that treat the Black community as an automatic base while neglecting the economic concerns of white and Hispanic working-class families. This is particularly important in a state with active Republican legislative presence: any future challenger will target the non-Black working-class voters specifically, and a legislator who has built relationships only within the Black community will find that flank vulnerable." },
          { name: "Unopposed Status as Consolidation Rather Than Permanence", tag: "careful", why: "Matthews' 2024 unopposed victory reflects genuine community support and effective political organization, but in South Carolina's Republican-controlled legislature, the absence of challenge indicates either legitimate Democratic dominance or insufficient perceived opportunity for Republican investment—the latter of which could change with a credible challenger or shifting state-level dynamics. Treating the unopposed margin as evidence of permanent Democratic control is strategically naive; it is better understood as a current consolidation that will be tested if Republican opposition ever organizes. Any successor or candidate should assume competitive challenge as a realistic scenario and build accordingly." },
          { name: "Treating the Black Constituency as Guaranteed While Neglecting White & Hispanic Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 43% Black population is the Democratic foundation of SC-SD-45, assuming Black voter turnout and support are automatic regardless of incumbent performance is the path to vulnerability. If a future challenger emerges with credible economic messaging—particularly on jobs and cost-of-living—and if the incumbent has engaged Black constituents episodically rather than sustained engagement on economic mobility, that consolidation can destabilize. The unopposed victory should not create overconfidence about the strength of Democratic hold in a working-class district where economic messaging can persuade across racial lines." },
          { name: "Abstract Identity or Social Messaging Without Economic Substance", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 28.6% renter rate, 21.4% college attainment, and $53,756 income, constituents are evaluating their legislator on whether government is delivering material improvements in their lives—not on abstract positioning on social issues or identity-based appeals divorced from economic outcomes. A legislator whose messaging emphasizes race-based rhetoric or social positioning without concrete constituent service on healthcare, jobs, and housing affordability is leaving the district vulnerable to a challenger who leads with economic message. This is particularly true for the white and Hispanic working-class voters whose support is electorally necessary: economic messaging that ignores their concerns in favor of race-specific appeals can activate them toward an alternative." }
